        <article class="about-modern-card about-modern-card--steps">
          <h3>How to Work with the Collection</h3>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Start broad:</strong> Search across all fields to identify promising titles and themes.</li>
            <li><strong>Narrow fast:</strong> Use collection filters to focus on monographs, serials, maps, or government content.</li>
            <li><strong>Validate context:</strong> Review publication details, date ranges, and source metadata before citation.</li>
            <li><strong>Move to evidence:</strong> Open the full document to read, compare, and extract primary-source passages.</li>
          </ol>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-modern-block" aria-labelledby="canadiana-faq-title">
      <div class="about-modern-block-head">
        <h3 id="canadiana-faq-title">Research Questions, Answered</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="about-modern-faq">
        <details open>
          <summary>What time period does the Canadiana Collection cover?</summary>
          <p>The collection includes materials from roughly 1500 to the present day, with especially strong coverage of pre-Confederation and early modern publication history.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How were materials selected for digitization?</summary>
          <p>Selection was guided by collection specialists and scholars, with emphasis on Canadian imprints, high-use historical sources, and materials that support broad interdisciplinary study.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I search inside documents?</summary>
          <p>Yes. Full-text and metadata search are available where text extraction exists, alongside title, creator, and subject searching.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is this collection suitable for teaching as well as research?</summary>
          <p>Yes. The range of formats and topics supports undergraduate instruction, graduate seminars, and advanced scholarly projects.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>
